What is a Microcontroller Unit?
A Microcontroller Unit (MCU) represents a compact integrated circuit designed to govern a specific operation in an embedded system. Unlike microprocessors found in personal computers that require external components, an MCU integrates a processor core, memory, and programmable input/output peripherals onto a single chip. This integration enables cost-effective digital control of devices and processes, making them indispensable components in everything from automobile engine controls and medical devices to smart appliances and industrial equipment.

As mixed-signal devices, many MCUs incorporate analog components necessary for interfacing with non-digital electronic systems. Their role has become increasingly crucial in the context of IoT, where they serve as economical edge devices for data collection, sensing, and actuating the physical world with localized intelligence.

